#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707958 :question/problem "Determine the domain of the real vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\frac{1}{x}\\,i+\\left(y^{2}\\right)\\,j.\n$$\n\nRemember: both component functions must be defined." :question/worked-solution "Write $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y)&=\\frac{1}{x}\\\\Q(x,y)&=y^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nP: Every denominator must be nonzero, including a square root in a denominator.\n\nThe required conditions are $x \\neq 0$.\n\nThus $D_P=\\left\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\,|\\,x \\neq 0\\right\\}$.\n\nQ: This polynomial is defined everywhere in the real plane.\n\nThus $D_Q=\\mathbb R^2$.\n\nThe domain is the intersection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D&=D_P\\cap D_Q\\\\&=\\left\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\,|\\,x \\neq 0\\right\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707964 :question/problem "Determine the domain of the real vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\sqrt{x - 1}\\,i+\\left(y^{2}\\right)\\,j.\n$$\n\nRemember: both component functions must be defined." :question/worked-solution "Write $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y)&=\\sqrt{x - 1}\\\\Q(x,y)&=y^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nP: A real square root requires a nonnegative radicand.\n\nThe required conditions are $x - 1 \\geq 0$.\n\nThus $D_P=\\left\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\,|\\,x - 1 \\geq 0\\right\\}$.\n\nQ: This polynomial is defined everywhere in the real plane.\n\nThus $D_Q=\\mathbb R^2$.\n\nThe domain is the intersection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D&=D_P\\cap D_Q\\\\&=\\left\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\,|\\,x - 1 \\geq 0\\right\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707980 :question/problem "The diagram shows the gradient vector field of a function $f(x,y)$. Which of the following could be a contour plot of $f$? Arrows show directions only.\n\n![Given nonradial outward gradient vector field](images/a5/a5ab7ab1e55a4c73fe75be5b4c82cab6ebec9404698487d510af7b5666c8160b.png)" :question/worked-solution "Level curves must cross the gradient vectors at right angles. Away from the axes, the arrows point more steeply upward or downward than radial arrows: near $(1,1)$ their direction is proportional to $(1,4)$, not $(1,1)$. Thus circles do not fit.\n\nThe matching curves are ellipses stretched horizontally. On their upper-right arcs the tangents slope gently downward, perpendicular to the steep upward-right arrows. Vertically stretched ellipses have the wrong tangent directions. The hyperbolas and parabolas also fail the perpendicularity check across the diagram.\n\n![Horizontally stretched ellipses and their normal vectors](images/95/955b541e62f4e6ecf05f19e58c36763d6f946961eafe877497a88de4fe450e5b.png)\n\nOne compatible function is $f(x,y)=x^2+4y^2$. Its contours $x^2+4y^2=c$ are horizontally stretched ellipses, and its gradient $(2x,8y)$ has the displayed directions. Values increase outward. Finding this formula is a check, not a required response."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707986 :question/problem "Which of the following could be the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)=y^2-x$? Use the relationship between level curves and gradient directions. Arrows show directions only." :question/worked-solution "First identify the level curves. Setting $f(x,y)=c$ gives\n\n$$\ny^2-x=c,\\qquad x=y^2-c.\n$$\n\nThese are right-opening parabolas. For example, $c=-1,0,1$ gives $x=y^2+1$, $x=y^2$, and $x=y^2-1$.\n\n![Labeled right-opening parabolic contours](images/a8/a886765cec9e09347d56dbfc948167ae35fd2fcae5a1a2907f4f7ed8464019de.png)\n\nFor fixed $y$, decreasing $x$ increases $f$, so the gradient must have a leftward component. Above the $x$-axis, increasing $y$ increases $y^2$, so the vectors point upward-left. Below the axis, decreasing $y$ increases $y^2$, so they point downward-left. On the axis they point directly left. These directions must also be perpendicular to the parabolas.\n\n![Gradient vectors normal to the parabolic contours and pointing toward larger labels](images/3e/3e635f50ea59c6f05af0b9f37f8f32fa15b7436ec93486b4a4cb7b7b6a2155dc.png)\n\nTherefore choose the field with leftward arrows on the $x$-axis, upward-left arrows above it, and downward-left arrows below it. A derivative check gives $\\nabla f=(-1,2y)$. The reversed field points toward decreasing values; the field that points down above the axis has the wrong vertical sign; the rotated parabolic field has the wrong contour orientation; the field tangent to the parabolas fails perpendicularity."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707988 :question/problem "The labeled lines are level curves of a function $f(x,y)$.\n\n![Labeled vertical level curves](images/c0/c0a3f9e3b2b3908d90ae86451d4054fe7f883fb29092753907d2111a79399cbb.png)\n\nRecall that gradient vectors are perpendicular to level curves and point toward increasing values of $f$. Which diagram could show the gradient vector field? Arrows show directions only." :question/worked-solution "Each level curve is vertical, so its gradient vectors must be horizontal. The labels increase from right to left: $-2,-1,0,1,2$. Therefore the gradient vectors point left, toward larger values of $f$.\n\n![Leftward vectors perpendicular to the labeled contours](images/f3/f3391d8805a64f907f1b854887370e94530e7cd278b7d730db9799a8ddb12217.png)\n\nThe correct diagram has leftward arrows everywhere. Rightward arrows point toward decreasing values; upward and downward arrows are tangent to the contours; diagonal arrows are not perpendicular."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708013 :question/problem "A vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$ assigns a vector in $\\mathbb R^3$ to each point of a subset of $\\mathbb R^3$.\n\nWhich of the following are vector fields on $\\mathbb R^3$? Use each function's natural real domain. Select all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$ assigns a vector in $\\mathbb R^3$ to each point of a subset of $\\mathbb R^3$.\n\nCheck each function's declared inputs and output vectors.\n\n- **T.** $T(x,y,z)$ takes points in $\\mathbb R^3$. Its three standard-basis coefficients give \n$$\nT(x,y,z)=\\langle \\sin(x), \\cos(y), \\cot(z)\\rangle.\n$$\nExclude the poles of the trigonometric component. Its natural domain is\n$$\nS_{T}=\\{(x,y,z)\\in \\mathbb R^3\\mid z\\ne k\\pi,\\ k\\in\\mathbb Z\\}.\n$$\nIt maps its nonempty domain in $\\mathbb R^3$ to vectors in $\\mathbb R^3$, so it **is** a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$.\n\n- **Q.** $Q(x,y)$ takes points in $\\mathbb R^2$. Its output has $2$ coordinates and is a vector in $\\mathbb R^2$. A denominator must be nonzero. Its natural domain is\n$$\nS_{Q}=\\{(x,y)\\in \\mathbb R^2\\mid x\\ne0\\}.\n$$\nIt **is not** a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$ because its declared inputs are not points of $\\mathbb R^3$ and its outputs are not vectors in $\\mathbb R^3$.\n\n- **Y.** $Y(x,y,z)$ takes points in $\\mathbb R^3$. Its output $\\sin(x)$ is a scalar in $\\mathbb R$. All components are defined on $\\mathbb R^3$. It **is not** a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$ because its outputs are not vectors in $\\mathbb R^3$.\n\nSelect $T$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708014 :question/problem "A vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$ assigns a vector in $\\mathbb R^3$ to each point of a subset of $\\mathbb R^3$.\n\nWhich of the following are vector fields on $\\mathbb R^3$? Use each function's natural real domain. Select all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$ assigns a vector in $\\mathbb R^3$ to each point of a subset of $\\mathbb R^3$.\n\nCheck each function's declared inputs and output vectors.\n\n- **M.** $M(x,y,z)$ takes points in $\\mathbb R^3$. Its output has $3$ coordinates and is a vector in $\\mathbb R^3$. All components are defined on $\\mathbb R^3$. It maps its nonempty domain in $\\mathbb R^3$ to vectors in $\\mathbb R^3$, so it **is** a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$.\n\n- **S.** $S(x,y)$ takes points in $\\mathbb R^2$. Its output has $2$ coordinates and is a vector in $\\mathbb R^2$. A logarithm needs a positive argument. A square root allows a zero radicand. Its natural domain is\n$$\nS_{S}=\\{(x,y)\\in \\mathbb R^2\\mid y-1>0,\\quad x-1\\ge0\\}.\n$$\nIt **is not** a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$ because its declared inputs are not points of $\\mathbb R^3$ and its outputs are not vectors in $\\mathbb R^3$.\n\n- **U.** $U(x,y,z)$ takes points in $\\mathbb R^3$. Its three standard-basis coefficients give \n$$\nU(x,y,z)=\\langle \\frac{1}{x+1}, y^2, \\frac{z}{y+1}\\rangle.\n$$\nA denominator must be nonzero. Its natural domain is\n$$\nS_{U}=\\{(x,y,z)\\in \\mathbb R^3\\mid x+1\\ne0,\\quad y+1\\ne0\\}.\n$$\nIt maps its nonempty domain in $\\mathbb R^3$ to vectors in $\\mathbb R^3$, so it **is** a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$.\n\nSelect $M$ and $U$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708019 :question/problem "A vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$ assigns a vector in $\\mathbb R^3$ to each point of a subset of $\\mathbb R^3$.\n\nWhich of the following are vector fields on $\\mathbb R^3$? Use each function's natural real domain. Select all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$ assigns a vector in $\\mathbb R^3$ to each point of a subset of $\\mathbb R^3$.\n\nCheck each function's declared inputs and output vectors.\n\n- **Y.** $Y(x,y,z)$ takes points in $\\mathbb R^3$. Its output has $2$ coordinates and is a vector in $\\mathbb R^2$. All components are defined on $\\mathbb R^3$. It **is not** a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$ because its outputs are not vectors in $\\mathbb R^3$.\n\n- **H.** $H(x,y,z)$ takes points in $\\mathbb R^3$. Its output $\\frac{1}{x+1}$ is a scalar in $\\mathbb R$. A denominator must be nonzero. Its natural domain is\n$$\nS_{H}=\\{(x,y,z)\\in \\mathbb R^3\\mid x+1\\ne0\\}.\n$$\nIt **is not** a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$ because its outputs are not vectors in $\\mathbb R^3$.\n\n- **N.** $N(x,y,z)$ takes points in $\\mathbb R^3$. Its three standard-basis coefficients give \n$$\nN(x,y,z)=\\langle \\sin(x), \\cos(y), \\cot(z)\\rangle.\n$$\nExclude the poles of the trigonometric component. Its natural domain is\n$$\nS_{N}=\\{(x,y,z)\\in \\mathbb R^3\\mid z\\ne k\\pi,\\ k\\in\\mathbb Z\\}.\n$$\nIt maps its nonempty domain in $\\mathbb R^3$ to vectors in $\\mathbb R^3$, so it **is** a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$.\n\nSelect $N$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708037 :question/problem "A vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$ assigns a vector in $\\mathbb R^3$ to each point of a subset of $\\mathbb R^3$.\n\nWhich of the following are vector fields on $\\mathbb R^3$? Use each function's natural real domain. Select all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$ assigns a vector in $\\mathbb R^3$ to each point of a subset of $\\mathbb R^3$.\n\nCheck each function's declared inputs and output vectors.\n\n- **Q.** $Q(t)$ takes one real parameter. Its output has $3$ coordinates and is a vector in $\\mathbb R^3$. All components are defined on $\\mathbb R$. It **is not** a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$ because its declared inputs are not points of $\\mathbb R^3$.\n\n- **L.** $L(x,y,z)$ takes points in $\\mathbb R^3$. Its output has $3$ coordinates and is a vector in $\\mathbb R^3$. Exclude the poles of the trigonometric component. Its natural domain is\n$$\nS_{L}=\\{(x,y,z)\\in \\mathbb R^3\\mid z\\ne k\\pi,\\ k\\in\\mathbb Z\\}.\n$$\nIt maps its nonempty domain in $\\mathbb R^3$ to vectors in $\\mathbb R^3$, so it **is** a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$.\n\n- **B.** $B(x,y,z)$ takes points in $\\mathbb R^3$. Its output has $3$ coordinates and is a vector in $\\mathbb R^3$. All components are defined on $\\mathbb R^3$. It maps its nonempty domain in $\\mathbb R^3$ to vectors in $\\mathbb R^3$, so it **is** a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$.\n\nSelect $L$ and $B$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708040 :question/problem "A vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$ assigns a vector in $\\mathbb R^3$ to each point of a subset of $\\mathbb R^3$.\n\nWhich of the following are vector fields on $\\mathbb R^3$? Use each function's natural real domain. Select all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$ assigns a vector in $\\mathbb R^3$ to each point of a subset of $\\mathbb R^3$.\n\nCheck each function's declared inputs and output vectors.\n\n- **H.** $H(x,y)$ takes points in $\\mathbb R^2$. Its output has $2$ coordinates and is a vector in $\\mathbb R^2$. A denominator must be nonzero. Its natural domain is\n$$\nS_{H}=\\{(x,y)\\in \\mathbb R^2\\mid x+1\\ne0\\}.\n$$\nIt **is not** a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$ because its declared inputs are not points of $\\mathbb R^3$ and its outputs are not vectors in $\\mathbb R^3$.\n\n- **Y.** $Y(x,y,z)$ takes points in $\\mathbb R^3$. Its three standard-basis coefficients give \n$$\nY(x,y,z)=\\langle \\ln(y), \\sqrt{x}, z^2\\rangle.\n$$\nA logarithm needs a positive argument. A square root allows a zero radicand. Its natural domain is\n$$\nS_{Y}=\\{(x,y,z)\\in \\mathbb R^3\\mid y>0,\\quad x\\ge0\\}.\n$$\nIt maps its nonempty domain in $\\mathbb R^3$ to vectors in $\\mathbb R^3$, so it **is** a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$.\n\n- **U.** $U(t)$ takes one real parameter. Its output has $3$ coordinates and is a vector in $\\mathbb R^3$. A logarithm needs a positive argument. A square root allows a zero radicand. Its natural domain is\n$$\nS_{U}=\\{(t)\\in \\mathbb R\\mid t+2>0,\\quad t+2\\ge0\\}.\n$$\nIt **is not** a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$ because its declared inputs are not points of $\\mathbb R^3$.\n\nSelect $Y$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708083 :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on $\\mathbb R^3$? Use each function's natural real domain. Select all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$ assigns a vector in $\\mathbb R^3$ to each point of a subset of $\\mathbb R^3$.\n\nCheck each function's declared inputs and output vectors.\n\n- **A.** $A(x,y,z)$ takes points in $\\mathbb R^3$. Its output has $3$ coordinates and is a vector in $\\mathbb R^3$. All components are defined on $\\mathbb R^3$. It maps its nonempty domain in $\\mathbb R^3$ to vectors in $\\mathbb R^3$, so it **is** a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$.\n\n- **R.** $R(x,y,z)$ takes points in $\\mathbb R^3$. Its output has $2$ coordinates and is a vector in $\\mathbb R^2$. A logarithm needs a positive argument. A square root allows a zero radicand. Its natural domain is\n$$\nS_{R}=\\{(x,y,z)\\in \\mathbb R^3\\mid y>0,\\quad x\\ge0\\}.\n$$\nIt **is not** a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$ because its outputs are not vectors in $\\mathbb R^3$.\n\n- **L.** $L(x,y,z)$ takes points in $\\mathbb R^3$. Its output has $2$ coordinates and is a vector in $\\mathbb R^2$. All components are defined on $\\mathbb R^3$. It **is not** a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$ because its outputs are not vectors in $\\mathbb R^3$.\n\nSelect $A$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708204 :question/problem "Find the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y,z)=x e^{xy-z}$.\n\nEnter its three component functions:\n\n$\\nabla f(x,y,z)=$ {{component-x}} $\\,i+$ {{component-y}} $\\,j+$ {{component-z}} $\\,k$." :question/worked-solution "Compute the partial derivatives, holding the other variables constant. In the $x$ derivative, both the factor $x$ and the exponent depend on $x$, so use the product rule as well as the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y,z)&=1\\cdot e^{xy-z}+x e^{xy-z}\\cdot y\\\\\n&=(1+xy)e^{xy-z},\\\\\nf_y(x,y,z)&=x e^{xy-z}\\cdot x=x^2e^{xy-z},\\\\\nf_z(x,y,z)&=x e^{xy-z}\\cdot(-1)=-x e^{xy-z}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the gradient vector field is\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y,z)=(1+xy)e^{xy-z}\\,i+x^2e^{xy-z}\\,j-xe^{xy-z}\\,k.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708213 :question/problem "Find the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y,z)=\\sin(xy-2z)$." :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field is $\\nabla f=f_x\\,i+f_y\\,j+f_z\\,k$.\n\nFor each partial derivative, differentiate the outer sine and multiply by the partial derivative of its argument. The other variables are held constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y,z)&=\\cos(xy-2z)\\cdot y=y\\cos(xy-2z),\\\\\nf_y(x,y,z)&=\\cos(xy-2z)\\cdot x=x\\cos(xy-2z),\\\\\nf_z(x,y,z)&=\\cos(xy-2z)\\cdot(-2)=-2\\cos(xy-2z).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y,z)=\\cos(xy-2z)\\left(y\\,i+x\\,j-2\\,k\\right).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708214 :question/problem "Find the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)=\\ln(3x-2y)$ on $3x-2y>0$." :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field is $\\nabla f=f_x\\,i+f_y\\,j$. When taking each partial derivative, hold the other variable constant.\n\nBy the chain rule,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_x(x,y)&=\\frac{1}{3x-2y}\\cdot 3=\\frac{3}{3x-2y},\\\\\nf_y(x,y)&=\\frac{1}{3x-2y}\\cdot(-2)=-\\frac{2}{3x-2y}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y)=\\frac{3}{3x-2y}\\,i-\\frac{2}{3x-2y}\\,j.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708222 :question/problem "The diagram shows the gradient vector field of a function $f(x,y)$. Which of the following could be a contour plot of $f$? Arrows show directions only.\n\n![Given gradient directions](images/e5/e5b0e58975a56cfa2aa4523da04e3d7f0ad9b52e5cedf4e894e264351cefbd86.png)" :question/worked-solution "The field must be perpendicular to every regular level curve. One compatible function, used here to check the geometric match, is $f(x,y)=4\\left(x+1\\right)^2+y^2$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y)&=c\\\\4\\left(x+1\\right)^2+y^2&=c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThese level curves are ellipses stretched vertically. Values increase away from the center $(-1,0)$. Compare oblique points too: the two coordinate directions are weighted in the ratio $4:1$. Unlabeled contours do not specify which side has larger values; the required test for these choices is perpendicularity, including at off-axis points.\n\nA derivative check gives $\\nabla f=\\left\\langle 8\\left(x+1\\right),2y\\right\\rangle$. These directions are normal to the level curves and point toward increasing values. Other curve shapes have a different normal direction somewhere; a reversed field, if offered, points downhill.\n\n![Matching contours and gradient directions](images/bf/bf2ea7634824a019093c9f09fd33da405efd49ed0154fe78003c9e0867ef13e8.png)\n\nTherefore choose this diagram:\n\n![Correct diagram](images/3d/3db952065d5ecfade2b25e8a240b05bd876758f0213dfae7f1e436e230eb0d5b.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708224 :question/problem "The diagram shows the gradient vector field of a function $f(x,y)$. Which of the following could be a contour plot of $f$? Arrows show directions only.\n\n![Given gradient directions](images/85/85f3b3ff78aeafd15ceed7338d0a9ae5b532a09e29b248ffda9048aa102e5ab6.png)" :question/worked-solution "The field must be perpendicular to every regular level curve. One compatible function, used here to check the geometric match, is $f(x,y)=y^2-\\left(x+1\\right)$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y)&=c\\\\y^2-\\left(x+1\\right)&=c\\\\x&=-1-c+y^2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThese level curves are right-opening parabolas. With $y$ fixed, moving left increases the function. With $x$ fixed, moving away from $y=0$ increases it. Unlabeled contours do not specify which side has larger values; the required test for these choices is perpendicularity, including at off-axis points.\n\nA derivative check gives $\\nabla f=\\left\\langle -1,2y\\right\\rangle$. These directions are normal to the level curves and point toward increasing values. Other curve shapes have a different normal direction somewhere; a reversed field, if offered, points downhill.\n\n![Matching contours and gradient directions](images/08/08b91a07aedb6c0c5a96016aa78dd81527f6f26096783bdd172135aede44373a.png)\n\nTherefore choose this diagram:\n\n![Correct diagram](images/39/398102b2c33d99162a9b99f74b9c8db5b8b4cd84715353c457e58a742c4c17b1.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708229 :question/problem "The diagram shows the gradient vector field of a function $f(x,y)$. Which of the following could be a contour plot of $f$? Arrows show directions only.\n\n![Given gradient directions](images/ef/ef0f85d081d7330f401f02afbf75847cdcbc9162a6349c819b2e629423581b18.png)" :question/worked-solution "The field must be perpendicular to every regular level curve. One compatible function, used here to check the geometric match, is $f(x,y)=x$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y)&=c\\\\x&=c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThese level curves are parallel straight lines. The labels increase in the normal direction proportional to $(1,0)$. A perpendicular field pointing the opposite way would decrease the function. Unlabeled contours do not specify which side has larger values; the required test for these choices is perpendicularity, including at off-axis points.\n\nA derivative check gives $\\nabla f=\\left\\langle 1,0\\right\\rangle$. These directions are normal to the level curves and point toward increasing values. Other curve shapes have a different normal direction somewhere; a reversed field, if offered, points downhill.\n\n![Matching contours and gradient directions](images/c1/c166d6c0617b4d39620eb32d41403ce2019c035f1adf62070b3ee4dc3be2e0e0.png)\n\nTherefore choose this diagram:\n\n![Correct diagram](images/52/522f72787a12f92cde753ff2bdd86e4bd53e62c995ad29c861b96e3c0ddb22ab.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708238 :question/problem "The diagram shows the gradient vector field of a function $f(x,y)$. Which of the following could be a contour plot of $f$? Arrows show directions only.\n\n![Given gradient directions](images/9c/9cb5ccbef95814c1532b53f0b7a55721ad4a6520dbfe0925d0ce7def8493270c.png)" :question/worked-solution "The field must be perpendicular to every regular level curve. One compatible function, used here to check the geometric match, is $f(x,y)=y^2+\\left(x+1\\right)$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y)&=c\\\\y^2+\\left(x+1\\right)&=c\\\\x&=-1+c-y^2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThese level curves are left-opening parabolas. With $y$ fixed, moving right increases the function. With $x$ fixed, moving away from $y=0$ increases it. Unlabeled contours do not specify which side has larger values; the required test for these choices is perpendicularity, including at off-axis points.\n\nA derivative check gives $\\nabla f=\\left\\langle 1,2y\\right\\rangle$. These directions are normal to the level curves and point toward increasing values. Other curve shapes have a different normal direction somewhere; a reversed field, if offered, points downhill.\n\n![Matching contours and gradient directions](images/03/030525a797176c6e01052f0f3fdefb27bb764f7a821984ab953f0ab96c58aa02.png)\n\nTherefore choose this diagram:\n\n![Correct diagram](images/78/78e298990e4de0dbc58883097dd88fcbe5338b8169ca34d166c7a395f73eb177.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708241 :question/problem "The diagram shows the gradient vector field of a function $f(x,y)$. Which of the following could be a contour plot of $f$? Arrows show directions only.\n\n![Given gradient directions](images/49/49c2c6963704418fd13bd01a5e822f141e025efecf2fd89d17f9efc9e2065de2.png)" :question/worked-solution "The field must be perpendicular to every regular level curve. One compatible function, used here to check the geometric match, is $f(x,y)=\\left(x+1\\right)y$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y)&=c\\\\\\left(x+1\\right)y&=c\\\\y&=\\frac{c}{\\left(x+1\\right)}\\quad(c\\ne0)\\\\c=0&:\\quad \\left(x+1\\right)=0\\ \\text{or}\\ y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThese level curves are rectangular hyperbolas, with the two intersecting straight lines at the zero level. Relative to $(-1,0)$, increasing the product of the two coordinates increases the function. Unlabeled contours do not specify which side has larger values; the required test for these choices is perpendicularity, including at off-axis points.\n\nA derivative check gives $\\nabla f=\\left\\langle y,\\left(x+1\\right)\\right\\rangle$. These directions are normal to the level curves and point toward increasing values. Other curve shapes have a different normal direction somewhere; a reversed field, if offered, points downhill.\n\n![Matching contours and gradient directions](images/1d/1d5b1da48e12b87e3b37ed324fce6ef1b537194006d95e99c0631580996dc182.png)\n\nTherefore choose this diagram:\n\n![Correct diagram](images/96/96c710e06e5099240aa2c79b3fb3c857a05e6404e38b204d5cfa20a075c2518c.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708256 :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)=\\frac{x^2}{2}+y^2$ at the points $(-2,2)$, $(2,1)$, $(1,-2)$?\n\nEach orange dot marks the tail of an arrow. All diagrams use the same scale, with one grid square representing one unit in each direction." :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field is $\\nabla f=f_x\\mathbf{i}+f_y\\mathbf{j}$. First, take the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\nf_x(x,y)=x,\\qquad f_y(x,y)=2y.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y)=x\\mathbf{i}+2y\\mathbf{j}.\n$$\n\nSubstitute the coordinates of each starting point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f(-2,2)&=(-2)\\mathbf{i}+2(2)\\mathbf{j}=-2\\mathbf{i}+4\\mathbf{j} \\\\ \\nabla f(2,1)&=2\\mathbf{i}+2(1)\\mathbf{j}=2\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j} \\\\ \\nabla f(1,-2)&=\\mathbf{i}+2(-2)\\mathbf{j}=\\mathbf{i}-4\\mathbf{j}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $(-2,2)$ the arrow moves two units left and four units up. At $(2,1)$ it moves two units right and two units up; at $(1,-2)$ it moves one unit right and four units down. Several alternatives have the correct signs, so compare the component lengths as well as direction.\n\nPlace each vector with its tail at the corresponding point. The matching diagram is:\n\n![](images/04/045ca85388276dfe022d4d028856472f24bc004ceda394bee29222cc1f386d1d.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708263 :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)=\\frac{(x-y)^2}{2}$ at the points $(2,1)$, $(1,2)$, $(1,-1)$?\n\nEach orange dot marks the tail of an arrow. All diagrams use the same scale, with one grid square representing one unit in each direction." :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field is $\\nabla f=f_x\\mathbf{i}+f_y\\mathbf{j}$. First, take the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(x,y)&=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot2(x-y)\\cdot1=x-y \\\\ f_y(x,y)&=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot2(x-y)\\cdot(-1)=-(x-y)\\end{aligned}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y)=(x-y)\\mathbf{i}-(x-y)\\mathbf{j}.\n$$\n\nSubstitute the coordinates of each starting point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f(2,1)&=(2-1)\\mathbf{i}-(2-1)\\mathbf{j}=\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j} \\\\ \\nabla f(1,2)&=(1-2)\\mathbf{i}-(1-2)\\mathbf{j}=-\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j} \\\\ \\nabla f(1,-1)&=(1-(-1))\\mathbf{i}-(1-(-1))\\mathbf{j}=2\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first two arrows have equal lengths and opposite directions. The arrow at $(1,-1)$ points down and right and is twice as long as the arrow at $(2,1)$. Every alternative agrees with some of these arrows, so check the vectors at all three starting points.\n\nPlace each vector with its tail at the corresponding point. The matching diagram is:\n\n![](images/fc/fc607f6ab6ff769d832cbddc82fab73d845f8fc769fb319b55458cf09d2ad7db.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708273 :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the gradient vector field of $f(x,y)=x+2y$ at the points $(-2,2)$, $(2,2)$, $(2,-2)$?\n\nEach orange dot marks the tail of an arrow. All diagrams use the same scale, with one grid square representing one unit in each direction." :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field is $\\nabla f=f_x\\mathbf{i}+f_y\\mathbf{j}$. First, take the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\nf_x(x,y)=1,\\qquad f_y(x,y)=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y)=\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}.\n$$\n\nSubstitute the coordinates of each starting point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f(-2,2)&=\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j} \\\\ \\nabla f(2,2)&=\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j} \\\\ \\nabla f(2,-2)&=\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe gradient is constant: every arrow moves one unit to the right and two units up, regardless of its starting point. An arrow moving two units right and one unit up interchanges the components.\n\nPlace each vector with its tail at the corresponding point. The matching diagram is:\n\n![](images/e9/e9b9bc65e813da9cb89f9bbabedc10a83b8da46ad13dfef03568ec204ee9e701.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708276 :question/problem "The labeled lines are level curves of a function $f(x,y)$.\n\n![Labeled level curves](images/f7/f710952e3423e09ae13bbd47c8943d2f274b042c341bc325403bf53fbb78f915.png)\n\nRecall that gradient vectors are **perpendicular** to level curves and point toward **increasing** function values. Which diagram could show its gradient vector field? Arrows show directions only." :question/worked-solution "The level curves are parallel straight lines. A normal is proportional to $(1,-1)$. Use the labels to choose its sense: moving in that direction raises the labels, so it is the gradient direction. Tangent arrows fail perpendicularity; the opposite normal points toward smaller labels.\n\n![Matching contours and gradient directions](images/fe/fe809367f4109420d7f8539abd69407066a052dc10c6b0afa1127ef66fc681c5.png)\n\nTherefore choose this diagram:\n\n![Correct diagram](images/11/11fd2ec491cdd866338cdee26092668315c872558b4b000608dd21f7e6dd701e.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708277 :question/problem "The labeled lines are level curves of a function $f(x,y)$.\n\n![Labeled level curves](images/cf/cfa0959b609a529164b19cc8e41475c9fc16a539d596a9b619a15e62cd8ba9c7.png)\n\nRecall that gradient vectors are **perpendicular** to level curves and point toward **increasing** function values. Which diagram could show its gradient vector field? Arrows show directions only." :question/worked-solution "The level curves are parallel straight lines. A normal is proportional to $(1,1)$. Use the labels to choose its sense: moving in that direction raises the labels, so it is the gradient direction. Tangent arrows fail perpendicularity; the opposite normal points toward smaller labels.\n\n![Matching contours and gradient directions](images/4d/4dc6a9ce9abbe5ef911453e1f5e5af6999d18ada4db453d2e3ad4821960e5912.png)\n\nTherefore choose this diagram:\n\n![Correct diagram](images/bb/bb83524be625efee137ead1baaddd2a81f0215f0c12a1c804fecbe939a135c00.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708291 :question/problem "The labeled lines are level curves of a function $f(x,y)$.\n\n![Labeled level curves](images/a7/a732abb535cef5034c6c08cd6f578758499f57284efcdf04b91ba6b6d2cf24d3.png)\n\nRecall that gradient vectors are **perpendicular** to level curves and point toward **increasing** function values. Which diagram could show its gradient vector field? Arrows show directions only." :question/worked-solution "The level curves are parallel straight lines. A normal is proportional to $(1,0)$. Use the labels to choose its sense: moving in that direction raises the labels, so it is the gradient direction. Tangent arrows fail perpendicularity; the opposite normal points toward smaller labels.\n\n![Matching contours and gradient directions](images/31/31a3379ef6ad1db6f6d7a5b0fbcf9e6bf3365f6bbef4d3aa6d93f03659c9b19c.png)\n\nTherefore choose this diagram:\n\n![Correct diagram](images/ef/ef0f85d081d7330f401f02afbf75847cdcbc9162a6349c819b2e629423581b18.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708299 :question/problem "The labeled lines are level curves of a function $f(x,y)$.\n\n![Labeled level curves](images/6d/6dd252f5ac4ae31575c0c3c88da96eb31dd8ffd57f30c939a68b68b084a7d6d0.png)\n\nRecall that gradient vectors are **perpendicular** to level curves and point toward **increasing** function values. Which diagram could show its gradient vector field? Arrows show directions only." :question/worked-solution "The level curves are parallel straight lines. A normal is proportional to $(-1,1)$. Use the labels to choose its sense: moving in that direction raises the labels, so it is the gradient direction. Tangent arrows fail perpendicularity; the opposite normal points toward smaller labels.\n\n![Matching contours and gradient directions](images/de/de4c7bf8194d85c42d462137191b2c3164ea65c6d3f802623cfd4f1949ca0bb9.png)\n\nTherefore choose this diagram:\n\n![Correct diagram](images/0c/0c17016c6470aeab549495c4cd7961ec7ad8ca4552fb1a12b0a6f377496f2101.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708439 :question/problem "![](images/60/6087da076e65bda5240c428ff67c570ad59ae9a8e5a5a3c9e8406184998de56a.png)\n\nThe path $C_1$ is the line segment from $O(0,0)$ to $A(1,0)$, and $C_2$ is the line segment from $A(1,0)$ to $B(0,-2)$. Traverse $C=C_1\\cup C_2$ in that order.\n\nLet $\\mathbf F(x,y)=(x+y)\\mathbf i+(2x-2y)\\mathbf j$. Given that\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=-\\frac{11}{2},\n$$\n\nevaluate \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\n$$\n\n.\n\nFor $C_1$, use $\\mathbf r_1(t)=t\\mathbf i+0\\mathbf j$, $0\\le t\\le1$." :question/worked-solution "Split the integral over the two oriented pieces:\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r+\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r.\n$$\n\nThe second integral is given, so calculate only the first. The supplied parametrization runs from $O$ to $A$. Along $C_1$, $x=t$ and $y=0$, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r_1(t))&=t\\mathbf i+2t\\mathbf j,\\\\\\mathbf r_1'(t)&=\\mathbf i,\\\\\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r_1(t))\\cdot\\mathbf r_1'(t)&=t(1)+2t(0)=t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=\\int_0^1t\\,\\mathrm dt=\\left[\\frac{t^2}{2}\\right]_0^1=\\frac12.\n$$\n\nAdd this to the supplied value for $C_2$:\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=\\frac12-\\frac{11}{2}=-5.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708440 :question/problem "![](images/87/87182d625f4e669a6b2791a17f3e12c34922043b8f3deefd6b2473e8fb529886.png)\n\nThe path $C_1$ is the line segment from $O(0,0)$ to $A(2,0)$, and $C_2$ is the section of the parabola $y=4-x^2$ from $A(2,0)$ to $B(0,4)$. Traverse $C=C_1\\cup C_2$ in that order.\n\nLet $\\mathbf F(x,y)=(x+y)\\mathbf i+2x\\mathbf j$. Given that\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=2,\n$$\n\nevaluate \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\n$$\n\n." :question/worked-solution "Use the supplied integral for the straight segment and calculate the curved contribution:\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r+\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r.\n$$\n\nTo use an increasing $x$ parameter, first parametrize the reversed curve $-C_2$, from $B$ to $A$:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=t\\mathbf i+(4-t^2)\\mathbf j,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le2.\n$$\n\nThis runs from $\\mathbf r(0)=(0,4)$ to $\\mathbf r(2)=(2,0)$, opposite to $C_2$. Along this curve,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))&=(t+4-t^2)\\mathbf i+2t\\mathbf j,\\\\\\mathbf r'(t)&=\\mathbf i-2t\\mathbf j,\\\\\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))\\cdot\\mathbf r'(t)&=(t+4-t^2)(1)+(2t)(-2t)\\\\&=4+t-5t^2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{-C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=\\int_0^2(4+t-5t^2)\\,\\mathrm dt\\\\&=\\left[4t+\\frac{t^2}{2}-\\frac53t^3\\right]_0^2\\\\&=8+2-\\frac{40}{3}=-\\frac{10}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReverse the sign to recover the required direction, then add the given contribution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=-\\int_{-C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=\\frac{10}{3},\\\\\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=2+\\frac{10}{3}=\\frac{16}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, $x=t$, $y=4-t^2$ with limits from $2$ to $0$ gives the required orientation directly."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708445 :question/problem "![](images/04/0457cb819e6f39ef9c0978e356cff788e1cf5edbef9cbf844a054ab777b70eac.png)\n\nThe path $C_1$ is the line segment from $A(-2,0)$ to $B(0,1)$, and $C_2$ is the line segment from $B(0,1)$ to $D(1,1)$. Traverse $C=C_1\\cup C_2$ in that order.\n\nLet $\\mathbf F(x,y)=(x-2y)\\mathbf i+(2x+y)\\mathbf j$. Given that\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=-\\frac32,\n$$\n\nevaluate \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\n$$\n\n." :question/worked-solution "Use additivity over the two pieces:\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r+\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r.\n$$\n\nThe second integral is given. Parametrize the first segment from $A$ to $B$ by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r_1(t)&=\\langle-2,0\\rangle+t\\bigl(\\langle0,1\\rangle-\\langle-2,0\\rangle\\bigr)\\\\&=(-2+2t)\\mathbf i+t\\mathbf j,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $t=0$ we are at $A$, and at $t=1$ we are at $B$, as required. Writing $\\mathbf F=P\\mathbf i+Q\\mathbf j$, substitute $x=-2+2t$ and $y=t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\mathbf r_1(t))&=(-2+2t)-2t=-2,\\\\Q(\\mathbf r_1(t))&=2(-2+2t)+t=-4+5t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r_1(t))&=-2\\mathbf i+(-4+5t)\\mathbf j,\\\\\\mathbf r_1'(t)&=2\\mathbf i+\\mathbf j,\\\\\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r_1(t))\\cdot\\mathbf r_1'(t)&=(-2)(2)+(-4+5t)(1)\\\\&=-8+5t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrate and then add the known contribution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=\\int_0^1(-8+5t)\\,\\mathrm dt\\\\&=\\left[-8t+\\frac52t^2\\right]_0^1=-\\frac{11}{2},\\\\\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=-\\frac{11}{2}-\\frac32=-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708455 :question/problem "![](images/06/06f90fbcd495493bcbab5bd9aa0109e89a6ac092aabb6949c3385bce4d543cb1.png)\n\nThe arrows show the orientations of $C_1$, $C_2$, and $C_3$. The closed path $C$ follows $A\\to B\\to C\\to A$ **counterclockwise**. For the same continuous vector field $\\mathbf F$, you are given\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=6,\\\\\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=-3,\\\\\\int_{C_3}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the integral along $C_2$ in the direction of its arrows.\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=\n$$\n\n {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The traversal $A\\to B$ reverses $C_1$, $B\\to C$ follows $C_2$, and $C\\to A$ reverses $C_3$. Thus, by additivity and orientation reversal,\n\n$$\n\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=-\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r+\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r-\\int_{C_3}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r.\n$$\n\nSubstitute the three given values:\n\n$$\n6=-(-3)+\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r-2=1+\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r.\n$$\n\nSubtract $1$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=5.\n$$\n\nThis is already the direction requested; no further reversal is needed."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708457 :question/problem "![](images/3e/3e0e8cf7cb700cb6d9ee074a0bb3eac1c82b24229b8d66e636121e53998fee7e.png)\n\nThe arrows show the orientations of the five segments. The same continuous vector field $\\mathbf F$ is used for every integral. Let $L_1$ follow $A\\to B\\to C\\to A$, and let $L_2$ follow $A\\to D\\to C\\to A$. You are given\n\n$$\n\\int_{L_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=3,\\qquad \\int_{L_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=-5.\n$$\n\nThe closed path $C$ follows the **outer boundary** $A\\to B\\to C\\to D\\to A$, counterclockwise. Evaluate its line integral.\n\n$$\n\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=\n$$\n\n {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Write $I_j=\\int_{C_j}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r$ for each segment in its arrow direction. The first triangular loop follows $C_1$, reverses $C_2$, and reverses the diagonal $C_5$. The second follows $C_4$, reverses $C_3$, and also reverses $C_5$. Hence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{L_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=I_1-I_2-I_5=3,\\\\\\int_{L_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=I_4-I_3-I_5=-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe outer boundary follows $C_1$, reverses $C_2$, follows $C_3$, and reverses $C_4$, so\n\n$$\n\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=I_1-I_2+I_3-I_4.\n$$\n\nSubtract the second loop integral from the first. This reverses the second loop; their diagonal contributions then cancel:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=(I_1-I_2-I_5)-(I_4-I_3-I_5)\\\\&=\\int_{L_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r-\\int_{L_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\\\\&=3-(-5)\\\\&=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two given loop integrals would traverse the diagonal twice in the same direction. Subtracting removes it. A closed path alone does not make a vector line integral zero."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708458 :question/problem "![](images/0a/0a6607e7239dda7b14a9164bb934556a0ac1a49778e791f6a3ceadebf6e11638.png)\n\nThe arrows show the orientations of $C_1$, $C_2$, and $C_3$. Let $C$ be the closed polygonal path $A\\to B\\to C\\to A$, traversed **counterclockwise**. Which expression equals\n\n$$\n\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\n$$\n\nfor an arbitrary continuous vector field $\\mathbf F$ on these segments?" :question/worked-solution "Follow the required traversal around the triangle. The segment $A\\to B$ follows $C_1$, the segment $B\\to C$ reverses $C_2$, and the segment $C\\to A$ follows $C_3$.\n\nAdditivity gives\n\n$$\n\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r+\\int_{-C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r+\\int_{C_3}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r\n$$\n\nReversing a path changes the sign of its vector line integral, so\n\n$$\n\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r-\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r+\\int_{C_3}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r.\n$$\n\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708524 :question/problem "Find the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y,z)=\\arctan{\\left(3 x y z \\right)} + 2$.\n\nEnter its component functions:\n\n$\\nabla f(x,y,z)=$ {{component-x}} $\\,i$ $+$ {{component-y}} $\\,j$ $+$ {{component-z}} $\\,k$." :question/worked-solution "Compute each partial derivative while holding the other variables constant. The additive constant has zero partial derivatives.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_{x}(x,y,z)&=\\frac{1}{1+(3 x y z)^2}\\cdot 3 y z \\\\ &=\\frac{3 y z}{9 x^{2} y^{2} z^{2} + 1} \\\\ f_{y}(x,y,z)&=\\frac{1}{1+(3 x y z)^2}\\cdot 3 x z \\\\ &=\\frac{3 x z}{9 x^{2} y^{2} z^{2} + 1} \\\\ f_{z}(x,y,z)&=\\frac{1}{1+(3 x y z)^2}\\cdot 3 x y \\\\ &=\\frac{3 x y}{9 x^{2} y^{2} z^{2} + 1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAssemble the components in coordinate order:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f(x,y,z)&=\\left(\\frac{3 y z}{9 x^{2} y^{2} z^{2} + 1}\\right)\\,i \\\\ &+\\left(\\frac{3 x z}{9 x^{2} y^{2} z^{2} + 1}\\right)\\,j \\\\ &+\\left(\\frac{3 x y}{9 x^{2} y^{2} z^{2} + 1}\\right)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708527 :question/problem "Find the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y,z)=z \\ln{\\left(- 2 x + y \\right)}$ on $- 2 x + y>0$.\n\nEnter its component functions:\n\n$\\nabla f(x,y,z)=$ {{component-x}} $\\,i$ $+$ {{component-y}} $\\,j$ $+$ {{component-z}} $\\,k$." :question/worked-solution "Compute each partial derivative while holding the other variables constant.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_{x}(x,y,z)&=z\\cdot\\frac{1}{- 2 x + y}\\cdot(-2) \\\\ &=- \\frac{2 z}{- 2 x + y} \\\\ f_{y}(x,y,z)&=z\\cdot\\frac{1}{- 2 x + y}\\cdot1 \\\\ &=\\frac{z}{- 2 x + y} \\\\ f_{z}(x,y,z)&=1\\cdot \\ln{\\left(- 2 x + y \\right)} \\\\ &=\\ln{\\left(- 2 x + y \\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAssemble the components in coordinate order:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f(x,y,z)&=\\left(- \\frac{2 z}{- 2 x + y}\\right)\\,i \\\\ &+\\left(\\frac{z}{- 2 x + y}\\right)\\,j \\\\ &+\\left(\\ln{\\left(- 2 x + y \\right)}\\right)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708533 :question/problem "Find the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y,z)=- 2 x^{4} z + x y^{2} - 3 y z^{3}$.\n\nEnter its component functions:\n\n$\\nabla f(x,y,z)=$ {{component-x}} $\\,i$ $+$ {{component-y}} $\\,j$ $+$ {{component-z}} $\\,k$." :question/worked-solution "Compute each partial derivative while holding the other variables constant.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_{x}(x,y,z)&=- 8 x^{3} z + y^{2} \\\\ &=- 8 x^{3} z + y^{2} \\\\ f_{y}(x,y,z)&=2 x y - 3 z^{3} \\\\ &=2 x y - 3 z^{3} \\\\ f_{z}(x,y,z)&=- 2 x^{4} - 9 y z^{2} \\\\ &=- 2 x^{4} - 9 y z^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAssemble the components in coordinate order:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f(x,y,z)&=\\left(- 8 x^{3} z + y^{2}\\right)\\,i \\\\ &+\\left(2 x y - 3 z^{3}\\right)\\,j \\\\ &+\\left(- 2 x^{4} - 9 y z^{2}\\right)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708537 :question/problem "Find the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y,z)=\\sin{\\left(x z - 2 y \\right)}$.\n\nEnter its component functions:\n\n$\\nabla f(x,y,z)=$ {{component-x}} $\\,i$ $+$ {{component-y}} $\\,j$ $+$ {{component-z}} $\\,k$." :question/worked-solution "Compute each partial derivative while holding the other variables constant.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_{x}(x,y,z)&=\\cos{\\left(x z - 2 y \\right)}\\cdot\\left(z\\right) \\\\ &=z \\cos{\\left(x z - 2 y \\right)} \\\\ f_{y}(x,y,z)&=\\cos{\\left(x z - 2 y \\right)}\\cdot\\left(-2\\right) \\\\ &=- 2 \\cos{\\left(x z - 2 y \\right)} \\\\ f_{z}(x,y,z)&=\\cos{\\left(x z - 2 y \\right)}\\cdot\\left(x\\right) \\\\ &=x \\cos{\\left(x z - 2 y \\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAssemble the components in coordinate order:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f(x,y,z)&=\\left(z \\cos{\\left(x z - 2 y \\right)}\\right)\\,i \\\\ &+\\left(- 2 \\cos{\\left(x z - 2 y \\right)}\\right)\\,j \\\\ &+\\left(x \\cos{\\left(x z - 2 y \\right)}\\right)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708539 :question/problem "Find the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{3 x^{2} + 3 y^{2} + 2 z^{2}} - 2$ on $(x,y,z)\\ne(0,0,0)$.\n\nEnter its component functions:\n\n$\\nabla f(x,y,z)=$ {{component-x}} $\\,i$ $+$ {{component-y}} $\\,j$ $+$ {{component-z}} $\\,k$." :question/worked-solution "Compute each partial derivative while holding the other variables constant. The additive constant has zero partial derivatives.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_{x}(x,y,z)&=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{3 x^{2} + 3 y^{2} + 2 z^{2}}}\\cdot 6 x \\\\ &=\\frac{3 x}{\\sqrt{3 x^{2} + 3 y^{2} + 2 z^{2}}} \\\\ f_{y}(x,y,z)&=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{3 x^{2} + 3 y^{2} + 2 z^{2}}}\\cdot 6 y \\\\ &=\\frac{3 y}{\\sqrt{3 x^{2} + 3 y^{2} + 2 z^{2}}} \\\\ f_{z}(x,y,z)&=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{3 x^{2} + 3 y^{2} + 2 z^{2}}}\\cdot 4 z \\\\ &=\\frac{2 z}{\\sqrt{3 x^{2} + 3 y^{2} + 2 z^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAssemble the components in coordinate order:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f(x,y,z)&=\\left(\\frac{3 x}{\\sqrt{3 x^{2} + 3 y^{2} + 2 z^{2}}}\\right)\\,i \\\\ &+\\left(\\frac{3 y}{\\sqrt{3 x^{2} + 3 y^{2} + 2 z^{2}}}\\right)\\,j \\\\ &+\\left(\\frac{2 z}{\\sqrt{3 x^{2} + 3 y^{2} + 2 z^{2}}}\\right)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708561 :question/problem "Find the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y,z)=- y \\sin{\\left(x - 2 y z \\right)}$.\n\nEnter its component functions:\n\n$\\nabla f(x,y,z)=$ {{component-x}} $\\,i$ $+$ {{component-y}} $\\,j$ $+$ {{component-z}} $\\,k$." :question/worked-solution "Compute each partial derivative while holding the other variables constant. In the $y$ derivative, both the coordinate prefactor and the argument depend on $y$, so use both the product rule and the chain rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_{x}(x,y,z)&=y\\cdot \\cos{\\left(x - 2 y z \\right)}\\cdot\\left(-1\\right) \\\\ &=- y \\cos{\\left(x - 2 y z \\right)} \\\\ f_{y}(x,y,z)&=- \\sin{\\left(x - 2 y z \\right)}+y\\cdot \\cos{\\left(x - 2 y z \\right)}\\cdot\\left(2 z\\right) \\\\ &=2 y z \\cos{\\left(x - 2 y z \\right)} - \\sin{\\left(x - 2 y z \\right)} \\\\ f_{z}(x,y,z)&=y\\cdot \\cos{\\left(x - 2 y z \\right)}\\cdot\\left(2 y\\right) \\\\ &=2 y^{2} \\cos{\\left(x - 2 y z \\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAssemble the components in coordinate order:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f(x,y,z)&=\\left(- y \\cos{\\left(x - 2 y z \\right)}\\right)\\,i \\\\ &+\\left(2 y z \\cos{\\left(x - 2 y z \\right)} - \\sin{\\left(x - 2 y z \\right)}\\right)\\,j \\\\ &+\\left(2 y^{2} \\cos{\\left(x - 2 y z \\right)}\\right)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708563 :question/problem "Find the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y,z)=z e^{x z - 2 y}$.\n\nEnter its component functions:\n\n$\\nabla f(x,y,z)=$ {{component-x}} $\\,i$ $+$ {{component-y}} $\\,j$ $+$ {{component-z}} $\\,k$." :question/worked-solution "Compute each partial derivative while holding the other variables constant. In the $z$ derivative, both the coordinate prefactor and the argument depend on $z$, so use both the product rule and the chain rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_{x}(x,y,z)&=z\\cdot e^{x z - 2 y}\\cdot\\left(z\\right) \\\\ &=z^{2} e^{x z - 2 y} \\\\ f_{y}(x,y,z)&=z\\cdot e^{x z - 2 y}\\cdot\\left(-2\\right) \\\\ &=- 2 z e^{x z - 2 y} \\\\ f_{z}(x,y,z)&=e^{x z - 2 y}+z\\cdot e^{x z - 2 y}\\cdot\\left(x\\right) \\\\ &=x z e^{x z - 2 y} + e^{x z - 2 y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAssemble the components in coordinate order:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f(x,y,z)&=\\left(z^{2} e^{x z - 2 y}\\right)\\,i \\\\ &+\\left(- 2 z e^{x z - 2 y}\\right)\\,j \\\\ &+\\left(x z e^{x z - 2 y} + e^{x z - 2 y}\\right)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186708568 :question/problem "Find the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y,z)=y e^{- 2 x + y z}$.\n\nEnter its component functions:\n\n$\\nabla f(x,y,z)=$ {{component-x}} $\\,i$ $+$ {{component-y}} $\\,j$ $+$ {{component-z}} $\\,k$." :question/worked-solution "Compute each partial derivative while holding the other variables constant. In the $y$ derivative, both the coordinate prefactor and the argument depend on $y$, so use both the product rule and the chain rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_{x}(x,y,z)&=y\\cdot e^{- 2 x + y z}\\cdot\\left(-2\\right) \\\\ &=- 2 y e^{- 2 x + y z} \\\\ f_{y}(x,y,z)&=e^{- 2 x + y z}+y\\cdot e^{- 2 x + y z}\\cdot\\left(z\\right) \\\\ &=y z e^{- 2 x + y z} + e^{- 2 x + y z} \\\\ f_{z}(x,y,z)&=y\\cdot e^{- 2 x + y z}\\cdot\\left(y\\right) \\\\ &=y^{2} e^{- 2 x + y z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAssemble the components in coordinate order:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f(x,y,z)&=\\left(- 2 y e^{- 2 x + y z}\\right)\\,i \\\\ &+\\left(y z e^{- 2 x + y z} + e^{- 2 x + y z}\\right)\\,j \\\\ &+\\left(y^{2} e^{- 2 x + y z}\\right)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
